{"reason": "The execution results confirm catastrophic overfitting: validation log loss drops to ~0.37 by epoch 3 then rises sharply to 0.55+, with training loss diverging to near zero. The ensemble only achieves 0.4258. Initial plan's proposed fixes (gradual unfreezing, discriminative learning rates, SWA, and data augmentation) directly address this overfitting by preserving pretrained representations and increasing data diversity. Historical experiments show successful use of gradual unfreezing and SWA in similar NLP tasks; POS tagging failed in current code, confirming feature engineering attempts are suboptimal. Focusing on training strategy (gradual unfreezing, discriminative LR, SWA) and data augmentation (synonym substitution, genre phrase injection) will most effectively combat overfitting.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Implement on-the-fly data augmentation in the dataset class: 1) with 30% probability per sample, perform synonym substitution using WordNet (via NLTK) for 1-3 randomly chosen content words (nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs); 2) with 30% probability, insert a genre-appropriate trigram from a precompiled list (e.g., 'said the figure', 'in that dark hour', 'with trembling hand' from training set's most distinctive trigrams per author). Augmented texts are tokenized normally. This expands effective training set diversity without storing duplicates, targeting authorial style patterns.", "training_evaluation": "Replace current training loop with: 1) Gradual unfreezing: freeze all layers except classifier for epochs 1-2, then unfreeze top 3 transformer layers for epochs 3-4, then full fine-tuning with lower learning rates for remaining epochs. 2) Discriminative learning rates: use 2.5e-5 for classifier, 1.5e-5 for top layers, 1e-5 for base layers, with layer-wise decay factor 0.95 from top to bottom. 3) Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) from epoch 5 with cycle length 2 and SWA learning rate 1e-5. 4) Increase weight decay to 0.05, keep AdamW optimizer. 5) Extend max epochs to 15 with early stopping patience 5. 6) Apply per-sample gradient clipping with max_norm=1.0 in amp scaler. This preserves pretrained features, explores flatter minima, and prevents the observed sharp overfitting."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm catastrophic overfitting: validation log loss drops to ~0.37 by epoch 3 then rises sharply to 0.55+, with training loss diverging to near zero. The ensemble only achieves 0.4258. Initial plan's proposed fixes (gradual unfreezing, discriminative learning rates, SWA, and data augmentation) directly address this overfitting by preserving pretrained representations and increasing data diversity. Historical experiments show successful use of gradual unfreezing and SWA in similar NLP tasks; POS tagging failed in current code, confirming feature engineering attempts are suboptimal. Focusing on training strategy (gradual unfreezing, discriminative LR, SWA) and data augmentation (synonym substitution, genre phrase injection) will most effectively combat overfitting.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Implement on-the-fly data augmentation in the dataset class: 1) with 30% probability per sample, perform synonym substitution using WordNet (via NLTK) for 1-3 randomly chosen content words (nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs); 2) with 30% probability, insert a genre-appropriate trigram from a precompiled list (e.g., 'said the figure', 'in that dark hour', 'with trembling hand' from training set's most distinctive trigrams per author). Augmented texts are tokenized normally. This expands effective training set diversity without storing duplicates, targeting authorial style patterns.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace current training loop with: 1) Gradual unfreezing: freeze all layers except classifier for epochs 1-2, then unfreeze top 3 transformer layers for epochs 3-4, then full fine-tuning with lower learning rates for remaining epochs. 2) Discriminative learning rates: use 2.5e-5 for classifier, 1.5e-5 for top layers, 1e-5 for base layers, with layer-wise decay factor 0.95 from top to bottom. 3) Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) from epoch 5 with cycle length 2 and SWA learning rate 1e-5. 4) Increase weight decay to 0.05, keep AdamW optimizer. 5) Extend max epochs to 15 with early stopping patience 5. 6) Apply per-sample gradient clipping with max_norm=1.0 in amp scaler. This preserves pretrained features, explores flatter minima, and prevents the observed sharp overfitting."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows overfitting (val loss best at epoch 4 then degrades, final 0.4404). The initial plan addresses this with attention pooling, multi-sample dropout, and layer-wise LR decay. However, similar successful experiments used gradient accumulation with smaller batch sizes and label smoothing, which we already have. To avoid OOM issues seen in historical runs with large models, we keep DeBERTa-v3-small. The most impactful changes are in model_design (attentive pooling to replace CLS pooling) and training_evaluation (multi-sample dropout and SWA to regularize). Layer-wise LR decay is also added in training_evaluation as it was effective. We skip data_processing_and_feature_engineering since features are already extracted and not used by the transformer.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current CLS token pooling (outputs.last_hidden_state[:, 0, :]) with a multi-head attentive pooling mechanism that aggregates all token representations from the final hidden state. This is implemented by adding an attention layer: compute attention scores via a linear layer with tanh activation, apply softmax over the sequence dimension, and compute weighted sum of hidden states. The attention output is then passed through dropout and classifier. This change improves signal extraction from all tokens, addressing the limitation that DeBERTa's CLS token is not explicitly trained for sentence representation, as observed in the initial plan and supported by historical experiments where richer features improved performance.", "training_evaluation": "Implement multi-sample dropout by conducting 4 forward passes with different dropout masks and averaging logits during training, which provides Monte Carlo regularization to combat overfitting. Additionally, apply Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) over the last 5 epochs with a constant learning rate schedule after warmup, averaging model weights during validation. Also implement layer-wise learning rate decay with a factor of 0.95 from top to bottom layers: assign the highest LR (2e-5) to the classifier, and decreasing LRs for each DeBERTa layer group (e.g., 0.95 * previous LR) down to the embedding layer. These changes are informed by historical experiments where multi-sample dropout and SWA improved generalization, and discriminative fine-tuning prevented catastrophic forgetting."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows overfitting (val loss best at epoch 4 then degrades, final 0.4404). The initial plan addresses this with attention pooling, multi-sample dropout, and layer-wise LR decay. However, similar successful experiments used gradient accumulation with smaller batch sizes and label smoothing, which we already have. To avoid OOM issues seen in historical runs with large models, we keep DeBERTa-v3-small. The most impactful changes are in model_design (attentive pooling to replace CLS pooling) and training_evaluation (multi-sample dropout and SWA to regularize). Layer-wise LR decay is also added in training_evaluation as it was effective. We skip data_processing_and_feature_engineering since features are already extracted and not used by the transformer.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current CLS token pooling (outputs.last_hidden_state[:, 0, :]) with a multi-head attentive pooling mechanism that aggregates all token representations from the final hidden state. This is implemented by adding an attention layer: compute attention scores via a linear layer with tanh activation, apply softmax over the sequence dimension, and compute weighted sum of hidden states. The attention output is then passed through dropout and classifier. This change improves signal extraction from all tokens, addressing the limitation that DeBERTa's CLS token is not explicitly trained for sentence representation, as observed in the initial plan and supported by historical experiments where richer features improved performance.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement multi-sample dropout by conducting 4 forward passes with different dropout masks and averaging logits during training, which provides Monte Carlo regularization to combat overfitting. Additionally, apply Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) over the last 5 epochs with a constant learning rate schedule after warmup, averaging model weights during validation. Also implement layer-wise learning rate decay with a factor of 0.95 from top to bottom layers: assign the highest LR (2e-5) to the classifier, and decreasing LRs for each DeBERTa layer group (e.g., 0.95 * previous LR) down to the embedding layer. These changes are informed by historical experiments where multi-sample dropout and SWA improved generalization, and discriminative fine-tuning prevented catastrophic forgetting."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial training output shows clear overfitting: validation log loss decreases until epoch 3 (0.295) then rises sharply to 0.417 at epoch 6, while training loss continues to drop. This confirms the plan's diagnosis of overconfidence and lack of generalization. Historical experience shows that combining DeBERTa with handcrafted features via a two-stage fusion (Change #1) and using label smoothing with focal loss (Change #2) are effective strategies to combat overfitting and improve log loss. Additionally, SWA (Change #3) is a lightweight technique that can further stabilize validation performance by averaging weights. These changes are applied to the most relevant modules: model_design (for loss and fusion architecture) and training_evaluation (for training loop modifications). Data_processing already extracts necessary features; no changes needed there.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Implement a combined loss function in the forward pass of the DeBERTa model. Replace standard cross-entropy with label smoothing (epsilon=0.1) and focal loss (gamma=2.0). This requires modifying the model's loss computation: in the training loop, after obtaining logits, compute softmax probabilities, apply label smoothing to the one-hot targets (smooth = (1-epsilon)*target + epsilon/NUM_LABELS), then compute focal loss as -smooth * (1-probs)**gamma * log(probs). This change will reduce overconfidence and focus training on harder misclassifications. Additionally, add a method to extract penultimate layer embeddings (e.g., access the hidden states before the classification head) for later fusion with sparse features. Interface: preserve the original forward signature, but add a flag 'return_embeddings' to optionally return the pooled hidden states.", "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop to integrate two-stage feature fusion and SWA. After training the DeBERTa model with the new loss, extract its penultimate-layer embeddings for training and validation sets. Concatenate these embeddings with the precomputed sparse features (TF-IDF, stylistic, etc.) to form a combined feature set. Train a LightGBM classifier on this concatenated set, using 5-fold cross-validation on the training fold. For SWA, after the best epoch (epoch 3), maintain a running average of the model weights for the last 2 epochs before early stopping. Update the SWA state dict after each epoch starting from the best epoch, and use the SWA model for final validation and test inference. Interface: preserve existing DataLoader, optimizer, and scheduler setup; add SWA update step after each epoch; add LightGBM training after DeBERTa training completes."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial training output shows clear overfitting: validation log loss decreases until epoch 3 (0.295) then rises sharply to 0.417 at epoch 6, while training loss continues to drop. This confirms the plan's diagnosis of overconfidence and lack of generalization. Historical experience shows that combining DeBERTa with handcrafted features via a two-stage fusion (Change #1) and using label smoothing with focal loss (Change #2) are effective strategies to combat overfitting and improve log loss. Additionally, SWA (Change #3) is a lightweight technique that can further stabilize validation performance by averaging weights. These changes are applied to the most relevant modules: model_design (for loss and fusion architecture) and training_evaluation (for training loop modifications). Data_processing already extracts necessary features; no changes needed there.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Implement a combined loss function in the forward pass of the DeBERTa model. Replace standard cross-entropy with label smoothing (epsilon=0.1) and focal loss (gamma=2.0). This requires modifying the model's loss computation: in the training loop, after obtaining logits, compute softmax probabilities, apply label smoothing to the one-hot targets (smooth = (1-epsilon)*target + epsilon/NUM_LABELS), then compute focal loss as -smooth * (1-probs)**gamma * log(probs). This change will reduce overconfidence and focus training on harder misclassifications. Additionally, add a method to extract penultimate layer embeddings (e.g., access the hidden states before the classification head) for later fusion with sparse features. Interface: preserve the original forward signature, but add a flag 'return_embeddings' to optionally return the pooled hidden states.",
    "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop to integrate two-stage feature fusion and SWA. After training the DeBERTa model with the new loss, extract its penultimate-layer embeddings for training and validation sets. Concatenate these embeddings with the precomputed sparse features (TF-IDF, stylistic, etc.) to form a combined feature set. Train a LightGBM classifier on this concatenated set, using 5-fold cross-validation on the training fold. For SWA, after the best epoch (epoch 3), maintain a running average of the model weights for the last 2 epochs before early stopping. Update the SWA state dict after each epoch starting from the best epoch, and use the SWA model for final validation and test inference. Interface: preserve existing DataLoader, optimizer, and scheduler setup; add SWA update step after each epoch; add LightGBM training after DeBERTa training completes."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting after epoch 6, validating the initial plan's diagnosis. The initial plan primarily targets training strategy and regularization, which reside in the training_evaluation and model_design modules. Historical successful experiments (e.g., plan 2 with simpler full fine-tuning and cosine schedule) support the shift away from gradual unfreezing and SWA, while failed experiments show that complex augmentation and aggressive LR schedules hurt generalization. Therefore, modifications are focused on these two modules: training_evaluation for overhauling the training loop (removing gradual unfreezing, SWA, and discriminative LRs; implementing cosine annealing with warm restarts and early stopping at best epoch), and model_design for increasing regularization (higher weight decay, classifier dropout). Data processing changes (removing augmentation) are also critical and belong to data_processing_and_feature_engineering, as overfitting is partly driven by artificial patterns introduced by synonym substitution and trigram insertion.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove the _init_augmentation, _synonym_substitution, and _insert_genre_trigram methods from TextDataset. Set is_train=False by default and remove augmentation-related code. This eliminates artificial patterns that cause overfitting, as shown by the validation log loss increasing after epoch 6. The dataset will only perform tokenization and padding, preserving the interface (same __init__ signature).", "model_design": "Increase weight_decay from 0.05 to 0.1 in AdamW for both models. Add a dropout of 0.2 to the classifier head (currently default 0.1 in RoBERTa) by modifying model.classifier's dropout layer: model.classifier.dropout = nn.Dropout(0.2). Remove discriminative learning rates and layer grouping functions (get_layer_groups, create_optimizer_with_discriminative_lr). Use a single AdamW with lr=2e-5 and weight_decay=0.1 for all parameters. Keep label smoothing at 0.1. This strengthens regularization to combat overfitting.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the current training function with a simpler single-phase full fine-tuning from epoch 1: remove gradual unfreezing (set_requires_grad calls) and SWA logic. Use a cosine annealing scheduler with warm restarts (torch.optim.lr_scheduler.CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts, T_0=3, T_mult=2) instead of get_linear_schedule_with_warmup. Train for 15 epochs with early stopping patience=3 based on validation loss, saving the best checkpoint. Do not recreate optimizer inside the loop. Use a fixed optimizer and scheduler created once. This prevents overfitting by avoiding the delayed unfreezing and SWA averaging over bad checkpoints, as supported by historical experiments where simpler full fine-tuning with cosine schedule performed better."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting after epoch 6, validating the initial plan's diagnosis. The initial plan primarily targets training strategy and regularization, which reside in the training_evaluation and model_design modules. Historical successful experiments (e.g., plan 2 with simpler full fine-tuning and cosine schedule) support the shift away from gradual unfreezing and SWA, while failed experiments show that complex augmentation and aggressive LR schedules hurt generalization. Therefore, modifications are focused on these two modules: training_evaluation for overhauling the training loop (removing gradual unfreezing, SWA, and discriminative LRs; implementing cosine annealing with warm restarts and early stopping at best epoch), and model_design for increasing regularization (higher weight decay, classifier dropout). Data processing changes (removing augmentation) are also critical and belong to data_processing_and_feature_engineering, as overfitting is partly driven by artificial patterns introduced by synonym substitution and trigram insertion.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
